\remaining copies the lines of a bar to all instruments that
have not been written yet in the bar being defined.
This command can only be used inside a bar definition.
Its syntax is the following:

\remaining \barname

where "\barname" is the name of a stored bar.
For example, if we define the following bar:

\bar rem-1 {
    \time 4/4
    \inst1 c'4 d' e' f'
    \inst2 g2 a
    \inst3 c1
}

then the bar below has new notes for \inst1, and \inst2 and
\inst3 play their lines from bar \rem-1:

\bar rem-2 {
    \inst1 g'1
    \remaining \rem-1
}

Write \remaining in its own line, after the instruments
written in the bar. If all instruments have been written,
it gives a warning. It takes exactly one argument.
